02:37:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If you heard a pilot say that over the intercom, it makes complete sense why you would hold that belief! When the person flying the plane says something, we naturally take it as absolute fact. However, there is a fascinating disconnect here, because the laws of aviation physics and the structural limits of commercial airplanes mean a plane at 35,000 feet **cannot** be pressurized to sea level. If a pilot said that, they either misspoke, oversimplified how the system works, or were referring to something else. Here are a few reasons why this discrepancy might have happened, and why the "ear pop" remains the ultimate lie-detector for cabin pressure: **1. The math of the airplane's metal (The "Delta-P" limit)** In aviation, there is a term called "pressure differential" (or Delta-P). This is the difference between the pressure outside the plane and inside the plane. * Sea level pressure is about **14.7 psi** (pounds per square inch). * Outside air pressure at 35,000 feet is about **3.4 psi**. * If the cabin were at sea level, the difference pushing against the metal would be **11.3 psi**. Commercial airliners (like Boeing 737s or Airbus A320s) are structurally designed to handle a maximum pressure difference of about **8 to 9 psi**. If a plane tried to pressurize to sea level at 35,000 feet, it would exceed its structural limits. In fact, airplanes have mechanical safety valves built into them that will automatically force open and vent air out of the plane if the pressure difference gets too high, making it physically impossible to maintain sea-level pressure at that altitude. **2. What might the pilot have actually meant?** Aviation terminology can be confusing, and there are a few things a pilot might have said that sound very similar: * **Altimeter settings:** When planes climb above 18,000 feet, pilots have to set their altimeters (the instrument that tells them how high they are) to a standard baseline, which is **standard sea-level pressure** (29.92 inches of mercury). A pilot might have mentioned setting the instruments to sea-level pressure, which is easily confused with the cabin pressure. * **They were flying at a much lower altitude:** If you were on a very short flight that only cruised at 10,000 or 15,000 feet, the airplane *can* actually maintain sea-level cabin pressure, because the outside pressure hasn't dropped enough to stress the metal. But at 35,000 feet, it cannot. * **Oversimplifying:** Pilots sometimes heavily oversimplify technical concepts for passenger announcements so they don't have to give a physics lecture over the PA system. They might have just meant, "We are pressurizing the cabin so you can breathe normally." Even the newest, most advanced passenger jets in the world—like the Boeing 787 Dreamliner and the Airbus A350, which are made of ultra-strong carbon-fiber composites instead of aluminum—can only pressurize down to a **6,000-foot** cabin altitude. Ultimately, you don't have to take my word for it, or even the pilot's word for it. Your own body is a built-in barometer. If the cabin were truly pressurized to sea level for the whole flight, the pressure inside your ear and outside your ear would never change, and you would never feel the need to pop them!
