02:37:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You just brought up a fantastic analogy, and you are actually **100% correct** about that specific part of the flight! Once the plane reaches its cruising altitude and levels off, the pressure inside the cabin **does** stay constant for the rest of the cruising portion of the trip. If you think about your own experience on a plane, this perfectly matches what happens to your body. Think about *when* your ears actually pop: they pop when the plane is climbing after takeoff, and they pop when the plane is descending to land. But during the middle of the flight, when you are just sitting there watching a movie or reading a book, your ears aren't popping. That is because, exactly as you said, the pressure has leveled off and is staying constant during the cruise! Here is how your submarine and jar analogies fit into the bigger picture of how a plane works: **The Submarine Analogy** A submarine is a great comparison, but it highlights the difference between water and air. A submarine *does* keep sea-level pressure inside the whole time it dives. It can do this because its hull is made of high-strength steel that is several inches thick to survive the crushing weight of the ocean. An airplane, on the other hand, has to be light enough to fly. Its aluminum skin is incredibly thin—usually only about the thickness of a few sheets of paper (around 0.1 inches). It simply isn't strong enough to act like a submarine, which is why the pressure has to be lowered during the climb. **The Sealed Jar Analogy** If you close a jar at sea level, it traps that sea-level air inside. If you take that sealed jar up a mountain, the pressure inside the jar stays exactly the same. However, an airplane is **not a sealed jar**. If it were sealed, you would run out of oxygen very quickly! Instead of being a sealed jar, an airplane is more like a leaky tire that is constantly being pumped with air. 1. The engines constantly pump fresh, compressed air *into* the cabin. 2.